"""Runtime controller: the bridge the shipped evaluators expect at ``context["controller"]``.

The 35 macOS evaluators were written against a controller object exposing::

    controller.run_python_script(script, timeout=...)  -> {"returncode", "stdout", "stderr"}
    controller.run_bash_script(script, timeout=...)    -> {"returncode", "stdout", "stderr"}
    controller.observe()                               -> observation with foreground_app / ui_nodes

The real MacUIBench HTTP runtime that is installed inside the guest
(``/Applications/MacUIBenchRuntime/runtime/runtime_server.py``) exposes a
*different* surface.  Verified live against the running runtime on port 5000::

    GET  /health              -> {"status": "HEALTHY", "capabilities": {...}}
    GET  /device_info         -> {"screen_width": 3360, "screen_height": 1418, ...}
    GET  /version             -> {"api": 2, "name": "macui-runtime"}
    GET  /observe             -> {"foreground_app", "foreground_bundle_id", "ui_nodes", ...}
    POST /run_python          <- {"code": ..., "timeout": ...}    (NOT "script")
    POST /run_bash_script     <- {"script": ..., "timeout": ...}
    POST /action              <- {"action": "tap", "target": ...}

Note the two contract differences this adapter absorbs:

* the endpoint is ``/run_python`` and the payload key is ``code`` (the
  evaluators call ``run_python_script(script=...)``);
* the runtime answers with ``{"status", "output", "error", "returncode"}`` while
  the evaluators read ``result["stdout"]`` / ``result["stderr"]``.

Authentication is a bearer token (``Authorization: Bearer <token>``); the token
file shipped with the runtime is ``/Applications/MacUIBenchRuntime/runtime/token.txt``.
"""

